1
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:05,118
Chapter 19.

2
00:00:05,118 --> 00:00:10,199
Morning made a considerable difference in my general prospect of Life, and brightened

3
00:00:10,199 --> 00:00:15,879
it so much that it scarcely seemed the same. What lay heaviest on my mind was,

4
00:00:15,879 --> 00:00:20,759
the consideration that six days intervened between me and the day of departure;

5
00:00:20,759 --> 00:00:26,559
for I could not divest myself of a misgiving that something might happen to London in the meanwhile,

6
00:00:26,559 --> 00:00:32,798
and that, when I got there, it would be either greatly deteriorated or clean gone.

7
00:00:32,798 --> 00:00:38,080
Joe and Biddy were very sympathetic and pleasant when I spoke of our approaching separation;

8
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:43,798
but they only referred to it when I did. After breakfast, Joe brought out my indentures from

9
00:00:43,798 --> 00:00:50,240
the press in the best parlour, and we put them in the fire, and I felt that I was free. With all

10
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:56,159
the novelty of my emancipation on me, I went to church with Joe, and thought perhaps the clergyman

11
00:00:56,159 --> 00:01:01,840
wouldn’t have read that about the rich man and the kingdom of Heaven, if he had known all.

12
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:06,958
After our early dinner, I strolled out alone, purposing to finish off the marshes at once,

13
00:01:06,958 --> 00:01:13,118
and get them done with. As I passed the church, I felt (as I had felt during service in the

14
00:01:13,118 --> 00:01:18,359
morning) a sublime compassion for the poor creatures who were destined to go there,

15
00:01:18,359 --> 00:01:24,438
Sunday after Sunday, all their lives through, and to lie obscurely at last among the low

16
00:01:24,438 --> 00:01:30,878
green mounds. I promised myself that I would do something for them one of these days, and

17
00:01:30,879 --> 00:01:36,319
formed a plan in outline for bestowing a dinner of roast-beef and plum-pudding, a pint of ale,

18
00:01:36,319 --> 00:01:41,118
and a gallon of condescension, upon everybody in the village.

19
00:01:41,118 --> 00:01:46,199
If I had often thought before, with something allied to shame, of my companionship with the

20
00:01:46,199 --> 00:01:53,159
fugitive whom I had once seen limping among those graves, what were my thoughts on this Sunday,

21
00:01:53,159 --> 00:02:00,519
when the place recalled the wretch, ragged and shivering, with his felon iron and badge!

22
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:05,958
My comfort was, that it happened a long time ago, and that he had doubtless been transported

23
00:02:05,958 --> 00:02:12,240
a long way off, and that he was dead to me, and might be veritably dead into the bargain.

24
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:19,000
No more low, wet grounds, no more dikes and sluices, no more of these grazing cattle,—though

25
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:25,080
they seemed, in their dull manner, to wear a more respectful air now, and to face round, in order

26
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:30,559
that they might stare as long as possible at the possessor of such great expectations,—farewell,

27
00:02:30,559 --> 00:02:36,919
monotonous acquaintances of my childhood, henceforth I was for London and greatness; not

28
00:02:36,919 --> 00:02:45,438
for smith’s work in general, and for you! I made my exultant way to the old Battery, and, lying

29
00:02:45,438 --> 00:02:52,400
down there to consider the question whether Miss Havisham intended me for Estella, fell asleep.

30
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:56,718
When I awoke, I was much surprised to find Joe sitting beside me, smoking

31
00:02:56,719 --> 00:03:03,719
his pipe. He greeted me with a cheerful smile on my opening my eyes, and said,—

32
00:03:03,719 --> 00:03:06,958
“As being the last time, Pip, I thought I’d foller.”

33
00:03:06,958 --> 00:03:09,879
“And Joe, I am very glad you did so.”

34
00:03:09,879 --> 00:03:11,359
“Thankee, Pip.”

35
00:03:11,359 --> 00:03:18,118
“You may be sure, dear Joe,” I went on, after we had shaken hands, “that I shall never forget you.”

36
00:03:18,118 --> 00:03:24,080
“No, no, Pip!” said Joe, in a comfortable tone, “I’m sure of that. Ay, ay, old chap! Bless you,

37
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:28,599
it were only necessary to get it well round in a man’s mind, to be certain on it. But it

38
00:03:28,599 --> 00:03:33,400
took a bit of time to get it well round, the change come so oncommon plump; didn’t it?”

39
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:38,919
Somehow, I was not best pleased with Joe’s being so mightily secure of me. I should have liked

40
00:03:38,919 --> 00:03:43,958
him to have betrayed emotion, or to have said, “It does you credit, Pip,” or something of that

41
00:03:43,958 --> 00:03:50,680
sort. Therefore, I made no remark on Joe’s first head; merely saying as to his second,

42
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:56,000
that the tidings had indeed come suddenly, but that I had always wanted to be a gentleman,

43
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,080
and had often and often speculated on what I would do, if I were one.

44
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:05,319
“Have you though?” said Joe. “Astonishing!”

45
00:04:05,319 --> 00:04:09,319
“It’s a pity now, Joe,” said I, “that you did not get on a little more,

46
00:04:09,319 --> 00:04:12,479
when we had our lessons here; isn’t it?”

47
00:04:12,479 --> 00:04:18,039
“Well, I don’t know,” returned Joe. “I’m so awful dull. I’m only master

48
00:04:18,039 --> 00:04:22,958
of my own trade. It were always a pity as I was so awful dull;

49
00:04:22,958 --> 00:04:28,919
but it’s no more of a pity now, than it was—this day twelvemonth—don’t you see?”

50
00:04:28,919 --> 00:04:34,559
What I had meant was, that when I came into my property and was able to do something for Joe, it

51
00:04:34,559 --> 00:04:40,638
would have been much more agreeable if he had been better qualified for a rise in station. He was so

52
00:04:40,638 --> 00:04:47,000
perfectly innocent of my meaning, however, that I thought I would mention it to Biddy in preference.

53
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,759
So, when we had walked home and had had tea, I took Biddy into our little garden by the

54
00:04:51,759 --> 00:04:57,400
side of the lane, and, after throwing out in a general way for the elevation of her spirits,

55
00:04:57,400 --> 00:05:02,479
that I should never forget her, said I had a favour to ask of her.

56
00:05:02,479 --> 00:05:09,240
“And it is, Biddy,” said I, “that you will not omit any opportunity of helping Joe on, a little.”

57
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:14,199
“How helping him on?” asked Biddy, with a steady sort of glance.

58
00:05:14,199 --> 00:05:19,159
“Well! Joe is a dear good fellow,—in fact, I think he is the dearest fellow that ever

59
00:05:19,159 --> 00:05:23,719
lived,—but he is rather backward in some things. For instance,

60
00:05:23,719 --> 00:05:30,400
Biddy, in his learning and his manners.” Although I was looking at Biddy as I spoke,

61
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:35,637
and although she opened her eyes very wide when I had spoken, she did not look at me.

62
00:05:36,199 --> 00:05:42,240
“O, his manners! won’t his manners do then?” asked Biddy, plucking a black-currant leaf.

63
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:45,319
“My dear Biddy, they do very well here—”

64
00:05:45,319 --> 00:05:50,758
“O! they do very well here?” interrupted Biddy, looking closely at the leaf in her hand.

65
00:05:50,759 --> 00:05:55,118
“Hear me out,—but if I were to remove Joe into a higher sphere, as I shall

66
00:05:55,118 --> 00:06:00,840
hope to remove him when I fully come into my property, they would hardly do him justice.”

67
00:06:00,840 --> 00:06:04,958
“And don’t you think he knows that?” asked Biddy.

68
00:06:04,958 --> 00:06:09,359
It was such a very provoking question (for it had never in the most distant

69
00:06:09,359 --> 00:06:13,679
manner occurred to me), that I said, snappishly,—

70
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:15,798
“Biddy, what do you mean?”

71
00:06:15,798 --> 00:06:20,400
Biddy, having rubbed the leaf to pieces between her hands,—and the smell of a

72
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:24,438
black-currant bush has ever since recalled to me that evening in the little garden by

73
00:06:24,438 --> 00:06:30,679
the side of the lane,—said, “Have you never considered that he may be proud?”

74
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:34,919
“Proud?” I repeated, with disdainful emphasis.

75
00:06:34,919 --> 00:06:38,039
“O! there are many kinds of pride,” said Biddy,

76
00:06:38,039 --> 00:06:44,000
looking full at me and shaking her head; “pride is not all of one kind—”

77
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,159
“Well? What are you stopping for?” said I.

78
00:06:47,159 --> 00:06:53,278
“Not all of one kind,” resumed Biddy. “He may be too proud to let any one take him out of a

79
00:06:53,278 --> 00:06:59,278
place that he is competent to fill, and fills well and with respect. To tell you the truth,

80
00:06:59,278 --> 00:07:05,519
I think he is; though it sounds bold in me to say so, for you must know him far better than I do.”

81
00:07:06,719 --> 00:07:12,798
“Now, Biddy,” said I, “I am very sorry to see this in you. I did not expect to see

82
00:07:12,798 --> 00:07:17,840
this in you. You are envious, Biddy, and grudging. You are dissatisfied

83
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:22,278
on account of my rise in fortune, and you can’t help showing it.”

84
00:07:22,278 --> 00:07:24,798
“If you have the heart to think so,” returned Biddy,

85
00:07:24,798 --> 00:07:30,000
“say so. Say so over and over again, if you have the heart to think so.”

86
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:34,919
“If you have the heart to be so, you mean, Biddy,” said I, in a virtuous and superior tone;

87
00:07:34,919 --> 00:07:40,438
“don’t put it off upon me. I am very sorry to see it, and it’s a—it’s a bad side of human

88
00:07:40,438 --> 00:07:46,558
nature. I did intend to ask you to use any little opportunities you might have after I was gone,

89
00:07:46,559 --> 00:07:52,599
of improving dear Joe. But after this I ask you nothing. I am extremely sorry

90
00:07:52,599 --> 00:07:59,559
to see this in you, Biddy,” I repeated. “It’s a—it’s a bad side of human nature.”

91
00:07:59,559 --> 00:08:03,240
“Whether you scold me or approve of me,” returned poor Biddy,

92
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:07,840
“you may equally depend upon my trying to do all that lies in my power, here,

93
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:13,798
at all times. And whatever opinion you take away of me, shall make no difference in my remembrance

94
00:08:13,798 --> 00:08:21,159
of you. Yet a gentleman should not be unjust neither,” said Biddy, turning away her head.

95
00:08:21,159 --> 00:08:26,719
I again warmly repeated that it was a bad side of human nature (in which sentiment,

96
00:08:26,719 --> 00:08:32,278
waiving its application, I have since seen reason to think I was right), and I walked down the

97
00:08:32,278 --> 00:08:38,598
little path away from Biddy, and Biddy went into the house, and I went out at the garden gate and

98
00:08:38,599 --> 00:08:46,240
took a dejected stroll until supper-time; again feeling it very sorrowful and strange that this,

99
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:52,159
the second night of my bright fortunes, should be as lonely and unsatisfactory as the first.

100
00:08:52,159 --> 00:08:56,159
But, morning once more brightened my view, and I extended my clemency to

101
00:08:56,159 --> 00:09:01,240
Biddy, and we dropped the subject. Putting on the best clothes I had,

102
00:09:01,240 --> 00:09:05,840
I went into town as early as I could hope to find the shops open, and presented myself

103
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:10,840
before Mr. Trabb, the tailor, who was having his breakfast in the parlour behind his shop,

104
00:09:10,840 --> 00:09:15,879
and who did not think it worth his while to come out to me, but called me in to him.

105
00:09:15,879 --> 00:09:20,199
“Well!” said Mr. Trabb, in a hail-fellow-well-met

106
00:09:20,199 --> 00:09:24,919
kind of way. “How are you, and what can I do for you?”

107
00:09:24,919 --> 00:09:30,278
Mr. Trabb had sliced his hot roll into three feather-beds, and was slipping butter in

108
00:09:30,278 --> 00:09:36,758
between the blankets, and covering it up. He was a prosperous old bachelor,

109
00:09:36,759 --> 00:09:41,039
and his open window looked into a prosperous little garden and orchard,

110
00:09:41,039 --> 00:09:45,919
and there was a prosperous iron safe let into the wall at the side of his fireplace,

111
00:09:45,919 --> 00:09:51,759
and I did not doubt that heaps of his prosperity were put away in it in bags.

112
00:09:51,759 --> 00:09:56,000
“Mr. Trabb,” said I, “it’s an unpleasant thing to have to mention,

113
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:00,680
because it looks like boasting; but I have come into a handsome property.”

114
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:06,400
A change passed over Mr. Trabb. He forgot the butter in bed, got up from the bedside,

115
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:12,558
and wiped his fingers on the tablecloth, exclaiming, “Lord bless my soul!”

116
00:10:12,559 --> 00:10:17,438
“I am going up to my guardian in London,” said I, casually drawing some guineas out of my pocket and

117
00:10:17,438 --> 00:10:23,000
looking at them; “and I want a fashionable suit of clothes to go in. I wish to pay for them,”

118
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:28,958
I added—otherwise I thought he might only pretend to make them, “with ready money.”

119
00:10:28,958 --> 00:10:34,080
“My dear sir,” said Mr. Trabb, as he respectfully bent his body, opened his arms,

120
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:39,958
and took the liberty of touching me on the outside of each elbow, “don’t hurt me by mentioning that.

121
00:10:39,958 --> 00:10:45,520
May I venture to congratulate you? Would you do me the favour of stepping into the shop?”

122
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:51,118
Mr. Trabb’s boy was the most audacious boy in all that country-side. When I had entered he

123
00:10:51,118 --> 00:10:57,080
was sweeping the shop, and he had sweetened his labours by sweeping over me. He was still sweeping

124
00:10:57,080 --> 00:11:01,680
when I came out into the shop with Mr. Trabb, and he knocked the broom against all possible

125
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:09,000
corners and obstacles, to express (as I understood it) equality with any blacksmith, alive or dead.

126
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,319
“Hold that noise,” said Mr. Trabb, with the greatest sternness,

127
00:11:13,319 --> 00:11:20,840
“or I’ll knock your head off!—Do me the favour to be seated, sir. Now, this,” said Mr. Trabb,

128
00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:25,798
taking down a roll of cloth, and tiding it out in a flowing manner over the counter,

129
00:11:25,798 --> 00:11:31,240
preparatory to getting his hand under it to show the gloss, “is a very sweet article. I

130
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:36,840
can recommend it for your purpose, sir, because it really is extra super. But you shall see some

131
00:11:36,840 --> 00:11:43,080
others. Give me Number Four, you!” (To the boy, and with a dreadfully severe stare; foreseeing

132
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:49,278
the danger of that miscreant’s brushing me with it, or making some other sign of familiarity.)

133
00:11:49,278 --> 00:11:54,118
Mr. Trabb never removed his stern eye from the boy until he had deposited number four on the

134
00:11:54,118 --> 00:11:59,919
counter and was at a safe distance again. Then he commanded him to bring number five,

135
00:11:59,919 --> 00:12:04,759
and number eight. “And let me have none of your tricks here,” said Mr. Trabb,

136
00:12:04,759 --> 00:12:10,359
“or you shall repent it, you young scoundrel, the longest day you have to live.”

137
00:12:10,359 --> 00:12:15,958
Mr. Trabb then bent over number four, and in a sort of deferential confidence recommended it to

138
00:12:15,958 --> 00:12:21,798
me as a light article for summer wear, an article much in vogue among the nobility and gentry,

139
00:12:21,798 --> 00:12:25,278
an article that it would ever be an honour to him to reflect upon a distinguished

140
00:12:25,278 --> 00:12:31,477
fellow-townsman’s (if he might claim me for a fellow-townsman) having worn. “Are you bringing

141
00:12:31,478 --> 00:12:35,241
numbers five and eight, you vagabond,” said Mr. Trabb to the boy after that,

142
00:12:35,840 --> 00:12:39,840
“or shall I kick you out of the shop and bring them myself?”

143
00:12:39,840 --> 00:12:44,599
I selected the materials for a suit, with the assistance of Mr. Trabb’s judgment,

144
00:12:44,599 --> 00:12:49,559
and re-entered the parlour to be measured. For although Mr. Trabb had my measure already,

145
00:12:49,559 --> 00:12:54,520
and had previously been quite contented with it, he said apologetically that it “wouldn’t

146
00:12:54,520 --> 00:13:01,719
do under existing circumstances, sir,—wouldn’t do at all.” So, Mr. Trabb measured and calculated me

147
00:13:01,719 --> 00:13:07,680
in the parlour, as if I were an estate and he the finest species of surveyor, and gave himself such

148
00:13:07,680 --> 00:13:12,438
a world of trouble that I felt that no suit of clothes could possibly remunerate him for his

149
00:13:12,438 --> 00:13:18,718
pains. When he had at last done and had appointed to send the articles to Mr. Pumblechook’s on the

150
00:13:18,719 --> 00:13:24,719
Thursday evening, he said, with his hand upon the parlour lock, “I know, sir, that London gentlemen

151
00:13:24,719 --> 00:13:30,199
cannot be expected to patronise local work, as a rule; but if you would give me a turn now and then

152
00:13:30,199 --> 00:13:37,879
in the quality of a townsman, I should greatly esteem it. Good-morning, sir, much obliged.—Door!”

153
00:13:37,879 --> 00:13:42,719
The last word was flung at the boy, who had not the least notion what it meant. But I saw

154
00:13:42,719 --> 00:13:48,199
him collapse as his master rubbed me out with his hands, and my first decided experience of

155
00:13:48,199 --> 00:13:55,719
the stupendous power of money was, that it had morally laid upon his back Trabb’s boy.

156
00:13:55,719 --> 00:14:01,199
After this memorable event, I went to the hatter’s, and the bootmaker’s, and the hosier’s,

157
00:14:01,199 --> 00:14:07,519
and felt rather like Mother Hubbard’s dog whose outfit required the services of so many trades.

158
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:12,879
I also went to the coach-office and took my place for seven o’clock on Saturday morning.

159
00:14:12,879 --> 00:14:17,478
It was not necessary to explain everywhere that I had come into a handsome property;

160
00:14:17,478 --> 00:14:22,240
but whenever I said anything to that effect, it followed that the officiating tradesman ceased to

161
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:26,919
have his attention diverted through the window by the High Street, and concentrated his mind

162
00:14:26,919 --> 00:14:33,359
upon me. When I had ordered everything I wanted, I directed my steps towards Pumblechook’s, and,

163
00:14:33,359 --> 00:14:38,598
as I approached that gentleman’s place of business, I saw him standing at his door.

164
00:14:38,599 --> 00:14:43,438
He was waiting for me with great impatience. He had been out early with the chaise-cart,

165
00:14:43,438 --> 00:14:48,718
and had called at the forge and heard the news. He had prepared a collation for me in the Barnwell

166
00:14:48,719 --> 00:14:55,958
parlour, and he too ordered his shopman to “come out of the gangway” as my sacred person passed.

167
00:14:55,958 --> 00:15:00,680
“My dear friend,” said Mr. Pumblechook, taking me by both hands, when he and I

168
00:15:00,680 --> 00:15:08,159
and the collation were alone, “I give you joy of your good fortune. Well deserved, well deserved!”

169
00:15:08,159 --> 00:15:13,319
This was coming to the point, and I thought it a sensible way of expressing himself.

170
00:15:13,319 --> 00:15:19,278
“To think,” said Mr. Pumblechook, after snorting admiration at me for some moments,

171
00:15:19,278 --> 00:15:24,598
“that I should have been the humble instrument of leading up to this, is a proud reward.”

172
00:15:24,599 --> 00:15:30,438
I begged Mr. Pumblechook to remember that nothing was to be ever said or hinted, on that point.

173
00:15:30,438 --> 00:15:37,438
“My dear young friend,” said Mr. Pumblechook; “if you will allow me to call you so—”

174
00:15:37,438 --> 00:15:43,958
I murmured “Certainly,” and Mr. Pumblechook took me by both hands again, and communicated

175
00:15:43,958 --> 00:15:47,199
a movement to his waistcoat, which had an emotional appearance, though it was

176
00:15:47,199 --> 00:15:54,278
rather low down, “My dear young friend, rely upon my doing my little all in your absence,

177
00:15:54,278 --> 00:15:59,758
by keeping the fact before the mind of Joseph.—Joseph!” said Mr. Pumblechook,

178
00:15:59,759 --> 00:16:04,919
in the way of a compassionate adjuration. “Joseph!! Joseph!!!”

179
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:12,159
Thereupon he shook his head and tapped it, expressing his sense of deficiency in Joseph.

180
00:16:12,159 --> 00:16:18,958
“But my dear young friend,” said Mr. Pumblechook, “you must be hungry, you must be exhausted. Be

181
00:16:18,958 --> 00:16:25,798
seated. Here is a chicken had round from the Boar, here is a tongue had round from the Boar,

182
00:16:25,798 --> 00:16:31,080
here’s one or two little things had round from the Boar, that I hope you may not despise. But

183
00:16:31,080 --> 00:16:36,240
do I,” said Mr. Pumblechook, getting up again the moment after he had sat down,

184
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:44,199
“see afore me, him as I ever sported with in his times of happy infancy? And may I—may I—?”

185
00:16:44,199 --> 00:16:48,879
This May I, meant might he shake hands? I consented,

186
00:16:48,879 --> 00:16:52,159
and he was fervent, and then sat down again.

187
00:16:52,159 --> 00:16:58,519
“Here is wine,” said Mr. Pumblechook. “Let us drink, Thanks to Fortune, and may she ever pick

188
00:16:58,520 --> 00:17:04,919
out her favourites with equal judgment! And yet I cannot,” said Mr. Pumblechook, getting up again,

189
00:17:05,559 --> 00:17:13,438
“see afore me One—and likewise drink to One—without again expressing—May I—may I—?”

190
00:17:13,438 --> 00:17:16,438
I said he might, and he shook hands with me again,

191
00:17:16,438 --> 00:17:21,838
and emptied his glass and turned it upside down. I did the same; and if I had turned

192
00:17:21,840 --> 00:17:27,118
myself upside down before drinking, the wine could not have gone more direct to my head.

193
00:17:27,118 --> 00:17:32,240
Mr. Pumblechook helped me to the liver wing, and to the best slice of tongue (none of those

194
00:17:32,240 --> 00:17:37,080
out-of-the-way No Thoroughfares of Pork now), and took, comparatively speaking,

195
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:44,118
no care of himself at all. “Ah! poultry, poultry! You little thought,” said Mr. Pumblechook,

196
00:17:44,118 --> 00:17:50,199
apostrophising the fowl in the dish, “when you was a young fledgling, what was in store for you. You

197
00:17:50,199 --> 00:17:55,879
little thought you was to be refreshment beneath this humble roof for one as—Call it a weakness,

198
00:17:55,879 --> 00:18:02,000
if you will,” said Mr. Pumblechook, getting up again, “but may I? may I—?”

199
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:06,359
It began to be unnecessary to repeat the form of saying he might, so he did it at

200
00:18:06,359 --> 00:18:13,080
once. How he ever did it so often without wounding himself with my knife, I don’t know.

201
00:18:13,080 --> 00:18:16,438
“And your sister,” he resumed, after a little steady eating,

202
00:18:16,438 --> 00:18:20,400
“which had the honour of bringing you up by hand! It’s a sad picter,

203
00:18:20,400 --> 00:18:26,199
to reflect that she’s no longer equal to fully understanding the honour. May—”

204
00:18:26,199 --> 00:18:29,719
I saw he was about to come at me again, and I stopped him.

205
00:18:29,719 --> 00:18:32,240
“We’ll drink her health,” said I.

206
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:37,558
“Ah!” cried Mr. Pumblechook, leaning back in his chair, quite flaccid with admiration,

207
00:18:37,559 --> 00:18:42,438
“that’s the way you know ’em, sir!” (I don’t know who Sir was, but he certainly was not I, and there

208
00:18:42,438 --> 00:18:48,080
was no third person present); “that’s the way you know the noble-minded, sir! Ever forgiving

209
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:54,319
and ever affable. It might,” said the servile Pumblechook, putting down his untasted glass in

210
00:18:54,319 --> 00:19:02,958
a hurry and getting up again, “to a common person, have the appearance of repeating—but may I—?”

211
00:19:02,958 --> 00:19:08,400
When he had done it, he resumed his seat and drank to my sister. “Let us never be

212
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:15,000
blind,” said Mr. Pumblechook, “to her faults of temper, but it is to be hoped she meant well.”

213
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:19,719
At about this time, I began to observe that he was getting flushed in the face;

214
00:19:19,719 --> 00:19:24,798
as to myself, I felt all face, steeped in wine and smarting.

215
00:19:24,798 --> 00:19:29,278
I mentioned to Mr. Pumblechook that I wished to have my new clothes sent to his house, and he was

216
00:19:29,278 --> 00:19:35,758
ecstatic on my so distinguishing him. I mentioned my reason for desiring to avoid observation in the

217
00:19:35,759 --> 00:19:42,400
village, and he lauded it to the skies. There was nobody but himself, he intimated, worthy

218
00:19:42,400 --> 00:19:48,958
of my confidence, and—in short, might he? Then he asked me tenderly if I remembered our boyish games

219
00:19:48,958 --> 00:19:55,080
at sums, and how we had gone together to have me bound apprentice, and, in effect, how he had

220
00:19:55,080 --> 00:20:01,477
ever been my favourite fancy and my chosen friend? If I had taken ten times as many glasses of wine

221
00:20:01,478 --> 00:20:07,080
as I had, I should have known that he never had stood in that relation towards me, and should in

222
00:20:07,080 --> 00:20:14,319
my heart of hearts have repudiated the idea. Yet for all that, I remember feeling convinced that

223
00:20:14,319 --> 00:20:21,039
I had been much mistaken in him, and that he was a sensible, practical, good-hearted prime fellow.

224
00:20:21,039 --> 00:20:26,159
By degrees he fell to reposing such great confidence in me, as to ask my advice in reference

225
00:20:26,159 --> 00:20:32,039
to his own affairs. He mentioned that there was an opportunity for a great amalgamation and monopoly

226
00:20:32,039 --> 00:20:37,638
of the corn and seed trade on those premises, if enlarged, such as had never occurred before in

227
00:20:37,638 --> 00:20:44,159
that or any other neighbourhood. What alone was wanting to the realisation of a vast fortune,

228
00:20:44,159 --> 00:20:52,598
he considered to be More Capital. Those were the two little words, more capital. Now it appeared to

229
00:20:52,599 --> 00:20:58,159
him (Pumblechook) that if that capital were got into the business, through a sleeping partner,

230
00:20:58,159 --> 00:21:04,039
sir,—which sleeping partner would have nothing to do but walk in, by self or deputy, whenever he

231
00:21:04,039 --> 00:21:10,240
pleased, and examine the books,—and walk in twice a year and take his profits away in his pocket,

232
00:21:10,240 --> 00:21:15,798
to the tune of fifty per cent,—it appeared to him that that might be an opening for a young

233
00:21:15,798 --> 00:21:21,359
gentleman of spirit combined with property, which would be worthy of his attention. But

234
00:21:21,359 --> 00:21:26,477
what did I think? He had great confidence in my opinion, and what did I think? I gave it as

235
00:21:26,478 --> 00:21:33,680
my opinion. “Wait a bit!” The united vastness and distinctness of this view so struck him,

236
00:21:33,680 --> 00:21:40,520
that he no longer asked if he might shake hands with me, but said he really must,—and did.

237
00:21:40,520 --> 00:21:46,000
We drank all the wine, and Mr. Pumblechook pledged himself over and over again to keep Joseph up to

238
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:52,400
the mark (I don’t know what mark), and to render me efficient and constant service (I don’t know

239
00:21:52,400 --> 00:21:58,758
what service). He also made known to me for the first time in my life, and certainly after having

240
00:21:58,759 --> 00:22:05,599
kept his secret wonderfully well, that he had always said of me, “That boy is no common boy,

241
00:22:05,599 --> 00:22:12,199
and mark me, his fortun’ will be no common fortun’.” He said with a tearful smile that

242
00:22:12,199 --> 00:22:20,278
it was a singular thing to think of now, and I said so too. Finally, I went out into the air,

243
00:22:20,278 --> 00:22:25,840
with a dim perception that there was something unwonted in the conduct of the sunshine, and

244
00:22:25,840 --> 00:22:31,278
found that I had slumberously got to the turnpike without having taken any account of the road.

245
00:22:31,278 --> 00:22:37,319
There, I was roused by Mr. Pumblechook’s hailing me. He was a long way down the sunny street,

246
00:22:37,319 --> 00:22:43,758
and was making expressive gestures for me to stop. I stopped, and he came up breathless.

247
00:22:43,759 --> 00:22:48,319
“No, my dear friend,” said he, when he had recovered wind for speech.

248
00:22:48,319 --> 00:22:52,159
“Not if I can help it. This occasion shall not entirely pass without that

249
00:22:52,159 --> 00:22:58,598
affability on your part.—May I, as an old friend and well-wisher? May I?”

250
00:22:58,599 --> 00:23:01,240
We shook hands for the hundredth time at least,

251
00:23:01,240 --> 00:23:04,519
and he ordered a young carter out of my way with the greatest indignation.

252
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:11,478
Then, he blessed me and stood waving his hand to me until I had passed the crook in the road;

253
00:23:11,478 --> 00:23:18,680
and then I turned into a field and had a long nap under a hedge before I pursued my way home.

254
00:23:18,680 --> 00:23:23,438
I had scant luggage to take with me to London, for little of the little I possessed was adapted

255
00:23:23,438 --> 00:23:29,679
to my new station. But I began packing that same afternoon, and wildly packed up things

256
00:23:29,680 --> 00:23:34,439
that I knew I should want next morning, in a fiction that there was not a moment to be lost.

257
00:23:35,400 --> 00:23:41,558
So, Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday, passed; and on Friday morning I went to Mr. Pumblechook’s,

258
00:23:41,559 --> 00:23:47,599
to put on my new clothes and pay my visit to Miss Havisham. Mr. Pumblechook’s own room was

259
00:23:47,599 --> 00:23:53,559
given up to me to dress in, and was decorated with clean towels expressly for the event. My

260
00:23:53,559 --> 00:23:59,118
clothes were rather a disappointment, of course. Probably every new and eagerly expected garment

261
00:23:59,118 --> 00:24:04,680
ever put on since clothes came in, fell a trifle short of the wearer’s expectation.

262
00:24:04,680 --> 00:24:09,400
But after I had had my new suit on some half an hour, and had gone through an immensity

263
00:24:09,400 --> 00:24:15,080
of posturing with Mr. Pumblechook’s very limited dressing-glass, in the futile endeavour to see my

264
00:24:15,080 --> 00:24:22,519
legs, it seemed to fit me better. It being market morning at a neighbouring town some ten miles off,

265
00:24:22,520 --> 00:24:27,879
Mr. Pumblechook was not at home. I had not told him exactly when I meant to leave,

266
00:24:27,879 --> 00:24:33,879
and was not likely to shake hands with him again before departing. This was all as it should be,

267
00:24:33,879 --> 00:24:40,039
and I went out in my new array, fearfully ashamed of having to pass the shopman, and suspicious

268
00:24:40,039 --> 00:24:46,278
after all that I was at a personal disadvantage, something like Joe’s in his Sunday suit.

269
00:24:46,278 --> 00:24:51,400
I went circuitously to Miss Havisham’s by all the back ways, and rang at the bell constrainedly,

270
00:24:51,400 --> 00:24:56,519
on account of the stiff long fingers of my gloves. Sarah Pocket came to the gate,

271
00:24:56,520 --> 00:24:59,958
and positively reeled back when she saw me so changed;

272
00:24:59,958 --> 00:25:04,840
her walnut-shell countenance likewise turned from brown to green and yellow.

273
00:25:04,840 --> 00:25:09,879
“You?” said she. “You? Good gracious! What do you want?”

274
00:25:09,879 --> 00:25:14,958
“I am going to London, Miss Pocket,” said I, “and want to say good-bye to Miss Havisham.”

275
00:25:14,958 --> 00:25:19,359
I was not expected, for she left me locked in the yard, while she went to ask if I were to be

276
00:25:19,359 --> 00:25:26,278
admitted. After a very short delay, she returned and took me up, staring at me all the way.

277
00:25:26,278 --> 00:25:30,879
Miss Havisham was taking exercise in the room with the long spread table, leaning on her crutch

278
00:25:30,879 --> 00:25:37,879
stick. The room was lighted as of yore, and at the sound of our entrance, she stopped and turned. She

279
00:25:37,879 --> 00:25:46,438
was then just abreast of the rotted bride-cake. “Don’t go, Sarah,” she said. “Well, Pip?”

280
00:25:46,438 --> 00:25:51,519
“I start for London, Miss Havisham, to-morrow,” I was exceedingly careful what I said,

281
00:25:51,520 --> 00:25:55,599
“and I thought you would kindly not mind my taking leave of you.”

282
00:25:55,599 --> 00:26:01,638
“This is a gay figure, Pip,” said she, making her crutch stick play round me, as if she,

283
00:26:01,638 --> 00:26:06,958
the fairy godmother who had changed me, were bestowing the finishing gift.

284
00:26:06,958 --> 00:26:10,159
“I have come into such good fortune since I saw you last,

285
00:26:10,159 --> 00:26:16,118
Miss Havisham,” I murmured. “And I am so grateful for it, Miss Havisham!”

286
00:26:16,118 --> 00:26:20,638
“Ay, ay!” said she, looking at the discomfited and envious Sarah,

287
00:26:20,638 --> 00:26:28,680
with delight. “I have seen Mr. Jaggers. I have heard about it, Pip. So you go to-morrow?”

288
00:26:28,680 --> 00:26:30,359
“Yes, Miss Havisham.”

289
00:26:30,359 --> 00:26:32,919
“And you are adopted by a rich person?”

290
00:26:32,919 --> 00:26:34,638
“Yes, Miss Havisham.”

291
00:26:34,638 --> 00:26:35,958
“Not named?”

292
00:26:35,958 --> 00:26:37,638
“No, Miss Havisham.”

293
00:26:37,638 --> 00:26:40,719
“And Mr. Jaggers is made your guardian?”

294
00:26:40,719 --> 00:26:42,638
“Yes, Miss Havisham.”

295
00:26:42,638 --> 00:26:46,958
She quite gloated on these questions and answers, so keen was her enjoyment

296
00:26:46,958 --> 00:26:53,359
of Sarah Pocket’s jealous dismay. “Well!” she went on; “you have a promising career before

297
00:26:53,359 --> 00:27:00,758
you. Be good—deserve it—and abide by Mr. Jaggers’s instructions.” She looked at me,

298
00:27:00,759 --> 00:27:08,118
and looked at Sarah, and Sarah’s countenance wrung out of her watchful face a cruel smile.

299
00:27:08,118 --> 00:27:12,359
“Good-bye, Pip!—you will always keep the name of Pip, you know.”

300
00:27:12,359 --> 00:27:13,758
“Yes, Miss Havisham.”

301
00:27:13,759 --> 00:27:15,478
“Good-bye, Pip!”

302
00:27:15,478 --> 00:27:22,240
She stretched out her hand, and I went down on my knee and put it to my lips. I had not

303
00:27:22,240 --> 00:27:26,240
considered how I should take leave of her; it came naturally to me at the

304
00:27:26,240 --> 00:27:32,080
moment to do this. She looked at Sarah Pocket with triumph in her weird eyes,

305
00:27:32,080 --> 00:27:38,159
and so I left my fairy godmother, with both her hands on her crutch stick, standing in

306
00:27:38,159 --> 00:27:44,840
the midst of the dimly lighted room beside the rotten bride-cake that was hidden in cobwebs.

307
00:27:44,840 --> 00:27:49,680
Sarah Pocket conducted me down, as if I were a ghost who must be seen out. She

308
00:27:49,680 --> 00:27:55,520
could not get over my appearance, and was in the last degree confounded. I said “Good-bye,

309
00:27:55,520 --> 00:28:00,638
Miss Pocket;” but she merely stared, and did not seem collected enough to know that

310
00:28:00,638 --> 00:28:06,080
I had spoken. Clear of the house, I made the best of my way back to Pumblechook’s,

311
00:28:06,080 --> 00:28:11,840
took off my new clothes, made them into a bundle, and went back home in my older dress,

312
00:28:11,840 --> 00:28:18,840
carrying it—to speak the truth—much more at my ease too, though I had the bundle to carry.

313
00:28:18,840 --> 00:28:25,559
And now, those six days which were to have run out so slowly, had run out fast and were gone,

314
00:28:25,559 --> 00:28:30,359
and to-morrow looked me in the face more steadily than I could look at it. As the

315
00:28:30,359 --> 00:28:37,039
six evenings had dwindled away, to five, to four, to three, to two,

316
00:28:37,039 --> 00:28:43,638
I had become more and more appreciative of the society of Joe and Biddy. On this last evening,

317
00:28:43,638 --> 00:28:50,240
I dressed myself out in my new clothes for their delight, and sat in my splendour until bedtime.

318
00:28:50,240 --> 00:28:55,840
We had a hot supper on the occasion, graced by the inevitable roast fowl, and we had some

319
00:28:55,840 --> 00:29:04,159
flip to finish with. We were all very low, and none the higher for pretending to be in spirits.

320
00:29:04,159 --> 00:29:10,080
I was to leave our village at five in the morning, carrying my little hand-portmanteau, and I had

321
00:29:10,080 --> 00:29:17,719
told Joe that I wished to walk away all alone. I am afraid—sore afraid—that this purpose originated

322
00:29:17,719 --> 00:29:24,478
in my sense of the contrast there would be between me and Joe, if we went to the coach together. I

323
00:29:24,478 --> 00:29:29,438
had pretended with myself that there was nothing of this taint in the arrangement; but when I went

324
00:29:29,438 --> 00:29:35,919
up to my little room on this last night, I felt compelled to admit that it might be so, and had

325
00:29:35,919 --> 00:29:42,400
an impulse upon me to go down again and entreat Joe to walk with me in the morning. I did not.

326
00:29:42,400 --> 00:29:48,400
All night there were coaches in my broken sleep, going to wrong places instead of to London,

327
00:29:48,400 --> 00:29:56,879
and having in the traces, now dogs, now cats, now pigs, now men,—never horses. Fantastic failures

328
00:29:56,879 --> 00:30:02,039
of journeys occupied me until the day dawned and the birds were singing. Then, I got up and

329
00:30:02,039 --> 00:30:09,118
partly dressed, and sat at the window to take a last look out, and in taking it fell asleep.

330
00:30:09,118 --> 00:30:13,759
Biddy was astir so early to get my breakfast, that, although I did not sleep at the window an

331
00:30:13,759 --> 00:30:19,199
hour, I smelt the smoke of the kitchen fire when I started up with a terrible idea that it must

332
00:30:19,199 --> 00:30:25,759
be late in the afternoon. But long after that, and long after I had heard the clinking of the teacups

333
00:30:25,759 --> 00:30:32,879
and was quite ready, I wanted the resolution to go downstairs. After all, I remained up there,

334
00:30:32,879 --> 00:30:38,240
repeatedly unlocking and unstrapping my small portmanteau and locking and strapping it up again,

335
00:30:38,240 --> 00:30:44,118
until Biddy called to me that I was late. It was a hurried breakfast with no taste in

336
00:30:44,118 --> 00:30:50,519
it. I got up from the meal, saying with a sort of briskness, as if it had only just occurred to me,

337
00:30:50,520 --> 00:30:55,599
“Well! I suppose I must be off!” and then I kissed my sister who was laughing and

338
00:30:55,599 --> 00:31:00,719
nodding and shaking in her usual chair, and kissed Biddy, and threw my arms around Joe’s

339
00:31:00,719 --> 00:31:07,919
neck. Then I took up my little portmanteau and walked out. The last I saw of them was,

340
00:31:07,919 --> 00:31:13,199
when I presently heard a scuffle behind me, and looking back, saw Joe throwing an old shoe after

341
00:31:13,199 --> 00:31:20,159
me and Biddy throwing another old shoe. I stopped then, to wave my hat, and dear old Joe waved his

342
00:31:20,159 --> 00:31:27,638
strong right arm above his head, crying huskily “Hooroar!” and Biddy put her apron to her face.

343
00:31:27,638 --> 00:31:32,879
I walked away at a good pace, thinking it was easier to go than I had supposed it would be,

344
00:31:32,879 --> 00:31:37,638
and reflecting that it would never have done to have had an old shoe thrown after the coach,

345
00:31:37,638 --> 00:31:45,278
in sight of all the High Street. I whistled and made nothing of going. But the village was very

346
00:31:45,278 --> 00:31:52,039
peaceful and quiet, and the light mists were solemnly rising, as if to show me the world,

347
00:31:52,039 --> 00:31:58,438
and I had been so innocent and little there, and all beyond was so unknown and great,

348
00:31:58,438 --> 00:32:04,319
that in a moment with a strong heave and sob I broke into tears. It was by the finger-post

349
00:32:04,319 --> 00:32:12,519
at the end of the village, and I laid my hand upon it, and said, “Good-bye, O my dear, dear friend!”

350
00:32:12,520 --> 00:32:17,719
Heaven knows we need never be ashamed of our tears, for they are rain upon the blinding dust

351
00:32:17,719 --> 00:32:26,199
of earth, overlying our hard hearts. I was better after I had cried than before,—more sorry, more

352
00:32:26,199 --> 00:32:34,199
aware of my own ingratitude, more gentle. If I had cried before, I should have had Joe with me then.

353
00:32:34,199 --> 00:32:39,319
So subdued I was by those tears, and by their breaking out again in the course of the quiet

354
00:32:39,319 --> 00:32:45,637
walk, that when I was on the coach, and it was clear of the town, I deliberated with an aching

355
00:32:45,638 --> 00:32:50,798
heart whether I would not get down when we changed horses and walk back, and have another evening at

356
00:32:50,798 --> 00:32:57,919
home, and a better parting. We changed, and I had not made up my mind, and still reflected

357
00:32:57,919 --> 00:33:03,840
for my comfort that it would be quite practicable to get down and walk back, when we changed again.

358
00:33:04,759 --> 00:33:10,759
And while I was occupied with these deliberations, I would fancy an exact resemblance to Joe in some

359
00:33:10,759 --> 00:33:17,478
man coming along the road towards us, and my heart would beat high.—As if he could possibly be there!

360
00:33:17,478 --> 00:33:22,840
We changed again, and yet again, and it was now too late and too far to go back,

361
00:33:22,840 --> 00:33:31,798
and I went on. And the mists had all solemnly risen now, and the world lay spread before me.

362
00:33:31,798 --> 00:33:34,991
This is the end of the first stage of Pip’s expectations.
